  8. WHAT THIS REPORT EXERCISES
  This class is one of the eleven API controller tests in this module, and it is the workflow history
  member of the six that form the API level surface of the workflow engine, the others covering claim and
  unclaim, completion, completion with an outcome, business process creation and process instance deletion.
  Of those six it is the one that reads the engine's OWN audit trail rather than acting on the engine, so
  it is the read side of that surface. Its two test cases split the ad hoc case from the non ad hoc case,
  which is precisely the branch on which a history shape regression would surface, and that is what makes
  this a valuable row on which to close the folder.
  The REST contracts those controllers expose, meaning paths, verbs, request and response shapes and
  authentication behaviour, are preserved with no controller signature, request mapping or serialised field
  change. Deliberately, none of that contract is transcribed into this report: no path, no verb, no request
  body and no response body appears below, and neither does any process instance identifier, process
  definition key, history record field list or serialised payload, all of which the source of this class
  does contain. A report records WHICH test case existed, not what the endpoint looks like, and restating a
  preserved contract inside the evidence for it would add a second place for it to drift. That restraint
  matters more for a history read endpoint than for most, because the shape of what it returns IS the
  contract under preservation.
  This class extends EasyMockSupport, so this report is also part of the before picture for the mocking
  library whose floor advance carries the widest POM footprint in the whole migration: the installed
  release AND the intermediate release fail identically on the target runtime with an inaccessible object
  error raised from the library's own class proxy factory, and only a much later release succeeds.
  It carries the Spring JUnit 4 runner as well. The framework advances by patch level within its existing
  generation, with no generation change, and the test support artifact and that runner advance with it, so
  this report is the before picture for that advance too.
  Every assertion in this class is unmodified by the migration. The module declares junit, spring-test, h2
  and easymock as its testing dependencies, and zero static mocking or bytecode mocking frameworks; a
  fifth test scoped dependency, jmimemagic, is a MIME detection library rather than a testing framework.
